So the credits are just gonna
get tinier and tinier

240
00:10:51,367 --> 00:10:53,333
until the movie's over then.

241
00:10:54,867 --> 00:10:56,833
Well, movie, I sure hope
you can justify

242
00:10:56,867 --> 00:10:59,233
all this personnel in the film.

243
00:10:59,267 --> 00:11:01,633
Stupid universe,
quit expanding!

244
00:11:03,033 --> 00:11:06,067
Attack of
the killer peanut brittle.

245
00:11:07,367 --> 00:11:09,367
Wow. Big.

246
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:13,433
Hey, the same guy
who wrote the Messiah.

247
00:11:13,467 --> 00:11:15,967
Fred gebhardt wrote
the Messiah?

248
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:17,967
No, the name before--
handel--

249
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,867
[scoffs]
Never mind.

250
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,867
No. Come on.
You're just recycling names
at this point, movie.

251
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:25,367
I guess focus groups
demanded more credits.

252
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:27,367
What happened to the fluffy,
absorbent credits?

253
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:29,367
I liked those.

254
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:33,033
Today, the moon narrowly missed
hitting a man's eye
like a big pizza pie.

255
00:11:33,067 --> 00:11:36,300
Scientists believe
that's amore.

256
00:11:36,333 --> 00:11:39,300
Honey, get your moon model
out of the living room.

257
00:11:41,667 --> 00:11:43,933
They make them use
the doggie door.

258
00:11:43,967 --> 00:11:47,133
Sir, I've been testing
the pressure equipment
for the Mars project.

259
00:11:47,167 --> 00:11:49,867
Forget about that now, Chapman.
You leave tomorrow morning.

260
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:52,200
The general wants someone
with your experience.

261
00:11:52,233 --> 00:11:54,700
- I don't know whether
to feel flattered or not.
- Don't.

262
00:11:54,733 --> 00:11:56,233
What exactly
will I be looking for?

263
00:11:56,267 --> 00:11:58,867
- Uh, floating space monsters?
- [Chuckling]

264
00:11:58,900 --> 00:12:01,333
This is no joke, frank.
I wish it were.

265
00:12:01,367 --> 00:12:03,433
- Don't make me hurt you.
- Sorry, sir.

266
00:12:03,467 --> 00:12:05,667
You know how rumors
move around this base.

267
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:09,033
Well, that's one of the things
we hope that you'll dispel.

268
00:12:09,067 --> 00:12:11,133
- Well, you're the colonel.
- I'm the weird guy.

269
00:12:11,167 --> 00:12:12,633
I'll take a crack
at anything once.

270
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:15,033
I'm glad you said that, soldier.

271
00:12:15,067 --> 00:12:17,700
And that's about
all you'll get, frank--
one crack.

272
00:12:17,733 --> 00:12:19,500
All I need, sir.

273
00:12:19,533 --> 00:12:21,500
- ** [dramatic soundtrack music]
- Geez! Just blast off,
I guess.

274
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:24,667
Don't wait for a countdown
or anything. Geez!

275
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:27,300
Does this tepid little scene
really deserve...

276
00:12:27,333 --> 00:12:31,033
** [imitating dramatic
soundtrack music]

277
00:12:31,067 --> 00:12:34,867
18,000 miles out
at 2-7-0 degrees azimuth

278
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:37,533
and 4-7 degrees ecliptic.

279
00:12:37,567 --> 00:12:40,133
Speed: 4.6 miles per second.

280
00:12:40,167 --> 00:12:41,467
[Singsong voice]
I'm not listening.

281
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,567
- Check. Computer guidance on.
- [Button clicks]

282
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:45,733
- Stability course on.
- [Button clicks]

283
00:12:45,767 --> 00:12:48,167
- Directional rockets port off.
- [Button clicks]

284
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,700
- Directional rockets
starboard off.
- [Button clicks]

285
00:12:50,733 --> 00:12:52,033
- Test automatic.
- Check.

286
00:12:52,067 --> 00:12:54,867
- Party tape. Check.
- [Equipment beeping]

287
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:56,700
- That does it, captain.
- Honey.

288
00:12:56,733 --> 00:12:59,033
- You can relax a bit now.
- Sweetie.

289
00:12:59,067 --> 00:13:01,433
Takeoff's always the same.

290
00:13:01,467 --> 00:13:03,500
My heart pounds
like a sledgehammer.

291
00:13:03,533 --> 00:13:06,133
** [imitating beginning
of Peter Gabriel's
"sledgehammer"]

292
00:13:06,167 --> 00:13:08,233
- How about the screen?
- Sorry.

293
00:13:08,267 --> 00:13:09,633
[Buttons clicking]

294
00:13:09,667 --> 00:13:11,567
The moon is fudge-dipped.

295
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:15,600
[Chapman]
Never fails to fascinate me
from up here.

296
00:13:15,633 --> 00:13:17,400
You know, captain,

297
00:13:17,433 --> 00:13:18,967
every year of my life,

298
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,300
- I grow more
and more convinced...
- [Scoffs]

299
00:13:21,333 --> 00:13:23,900
That the wisest and best
is to fix our attention

300
00:13:23,933 --> 00:13:26,567
on the good
and on the beautiful--

301
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:29,533
- don't hit him.
- ...If you'll just take
the time to look at it.

302
00:13:29,567 --> 00:13:31,567
- You're some guy, makonnen.
- [Chuckles]

303
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:33,533
[Woman on radio]
Lunar base 1 to Pegasus iv.

304
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:42,900
Spaceship. Now with
gentle-glide applicator.

305
00:13:42,933 --> 00:13:46,067
What's our elapsed time
from base?

306
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:48,867
14 hours, 22 minutes,
and 30 seconds.

307
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:52,733
You could stay up here
another 14 hours
and still see nothing.

308
00:13:52,767 --> 00:13:54,967
- Even 1400 hours.
- Even 14 million.

309
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:56,700
That's about all
we can do, though.

310
00:13:56,733 --> 00:13:59,000
Are you sure
we're still on course?

311
00:13:59,033 --> 00:14:00,567
- Why?
- We're underwater.

312
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:02,567
Well, I have an idea.

313
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:05,600
- Turn to
2-7-8 degrees azimuth--
- you're mad!

314
00:14:05,633 --> 00:14:07,767
...0-47 degrees ecliptic.

315
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:09,267
Yes, sir.

316
00:14:09,300 --> 00:14:11,367
Changing course
to 2-7-8 degrees azimuth,

317
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:13,667
- ...hold 4-7 degrees ecliptic.
- [Beeps]

318
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:15,800
[Imitates beep]

319
00:14:15,833 --> 00:14:18,100
Lansfield tells US
to charter the same course

320
00:14:18,133 --> 00:14:19,767
Pegasus III took.

321
00:14:19,800 --> 00:14:21,867
Well, that's fine, but I
don't think we'll get anywhere.

322
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:24,400
- Knowing US.
- If this strange planet

323
00:14:24,433 --> 00:14:26,000
dashes about
like it's supposed to,

324
00:14:26,033 --> 00:14:28,533
it figures we won't find it
around here,

325
00:14:28,567 --> 00:14:31,067
so we're changing course.

326
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:33,333
Lightning never strikes twice
in the same place.

327
00:14:33,367 --> 00:14:35,233
Precisely. You with me?

328
00:14:35,267 --> 00:14:37,500
- Like I said,
you're the captain.
- Unfortunately.

329
00:14:37,533 --> 00:14:39,967
- I like the new reading.
- Thanks, ray.

330
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,467
You know, captain,
every year,

331
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,067
I think more and more
of the goodest

332
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:45,100
and the nicest and bestest
that we can be.

333
00:14:45,133 --> 00:14:47,033
Yeah, whatever, ray.

334
00:14:47,067 --> 00:14:49,233
Hey, it's an adult rocket ship.

335
00:14:49,267 --> 00:14:51,033
[Chuckling]

336
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:53,900
- You know, captain--
- shut up, ray!

337
00:14:53,933 --> 00:14:56,933
We could go nuts out here
just waiting for something
to happen.

338
00:14:56,967 --> 00:15:00,267
Wanna play asteroids?

339
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:03,100
- You seem pretty much at ease.
- You know, captain--

340
00:15:03,133 --> 00:15:05,133
well, I figure it's just
the same as fishing.

341
00:15:05,167 --> 00:15:06,667
You gotta be patient
and wait.

342
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,333
Drink lots of beer.

343
00:15:08,367 --> 00:15:09,667
[Alarm beeping]

344
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:11,500
The electrostatic meter's
going haywire.

345
00:15:11,533 --> 00:15:15,067
Lunar base 1 to Pegasus iv.
Can you read me? Over.

346
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:17,600
- Well, have you been published?
- Pegasus iv to lunar base 1.

347
00:15:17,633 --> 00:15:19,733
We read you. Over.

348
00:15:19,767 --> 00:15:22,067
You are completely
off course, Pegasus iv.

349
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:25,067
- Check your position
immediately.
- Or you'll get such a spanking.

350
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:27,500
- D computer and spectrometer
are out.
- [Beeping continues]

351
00:15:27,533 --> 00:15:29,633
Pegasus iv to lunar base 1.

352
00:15:29,667 --> 00:15:31,833
We are aware
of being off course.

353
00:15:31,867 --> 00:15:33,633
Cannot explain at present.

354
00:15:33,667 --> 00:15:35,833
We're entering
a heavy magnetic field.

355
00:15:35,867 --> 00:15:37,833
Several instruments are out,
and I'm afraid others are going.

356
00:15:37,867 --> 00:15:40,767
- And it's not my fault.
- Give US our exact position.

357
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,267
Over.

358
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:43,700
Pegasus iv, you are--
[Static]

359
00:15:43,733 --> 00:15:45,200
Completely boned.

360
00:15:45,233 --> 00:15:48,167
Cannot hear you, lunar base 1.
Over.

361
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:50,533
- [Static continues]
- He's so pretty.

362
00:15:50,567 --> 00:15:52,333
Changing to manual control.

363
00:15:52,367 --> 00:15:54,100
That's the good
and beautiful thing to do, sir.

364
00:15:54,133 --> 00:15:55,167
[Ray]
Meteors!

365
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:57,733
Ach!

366
00:15:57,767 --> 00:16:00,367
- Someone on the mir flushed.
- Ooh-hoo.

367
00:16:03,133 --> 00:16:05,367
You know, captain,
I'm scared!

368
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:09,133
- Boy, that was close.
- Yeah. And I'm afraid
it isn't over yet.

369
00:16:09,167 --> 00:16:12,967
Roving gangs of meteors
terrorize the galaxy.

370
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,400
Out of the way,
spaceship punk!

371
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:19,333
We're on a semi-closing course
with them.

372
00:16:19,367 --> 00:16:22,100
Screen one's fading fast.
What about
the infrared detector?

373
00:16:22,133 --> 00:16:24,200
- That's out too.
- The only thing we can do

374
00:16:24,233 --> 00:16:26,767
is turn it 9-0 degrees
to the shower's path.

375
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:28,867
More meteors are coming.
It's our only chance.

376
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:32,567
That will be
3-1-1 degrees azimuth

377
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:34,800
and minus 1-2 degrees ecliptic.

378
00:16:34,833 --> 00:16:38,500
Yeah, fine.
Just turn left.

379
00:16:38,533 --> 00:16:40,100
- Here they come.
- Prepare the milk.

380
00:16:40,133 --> 00:16:41,933
This is it.

381
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,100
- [Explosion]
- Doh!

382
00:16:45,133 --> 00:16:47,933
I filled my pants, sir.
In fact, I think I filled
yours too.

383
00:16:47,967 --> 00:16:49,600
[Chuckles]

384
00:16:52,767 --> 00:16:56,000
Suckers! We'll be back!

385
00:16:56,033 --> 00:16:58,367
Looks like the worst is over.

386
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:00,100
I see a lot of spots.

387
00:17:00,133 --> 00:17:02,400
Those are ionized trails.
They'll persist for awhile.

388
00:17:02,433 --> 00:17:05,167
Well, let's hope
there's no stragglers.

389
00:17:05,200 --> 00:17:07,067
With these instruments out,

390
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:09,567
there won't be any way
to tell if they're coming
from another direction.

391
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:11,433
Trick now is to get back
to the moon.

392
00:17:11,467 --> 00:17:13,833
Permission to talk
in flowery prose again, sir?

393
00:17:13,867 --> 00:17:15,833
- I think we'll make it.
- Yeah.

394
00:17:15,867 --> 00:17:17,833
I know you think that.

395
00:17:17,867 --> 00:17:19,667
Okay, ray, let's go through
a type-b check.

396
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:21,500
- All right.
- Hop up on the table.

397
00:17:21,533 --> 00:17:24,300
- Cabin pressure.
- 20, coming up.

398
00:17:24,333 --> 00:17:27,533
- Retro port.
- 20 port, sir.

399
00:17:27,567 --> 00:17:30,533
- Positive.
- Retro starboard.

400
00:17:30,567 --> 00:17:32,967
- Positive.
- Main circuit one.

401
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,333
- Good and beautiful.
- Check.

402
00:17:36,367 --> 00:17:38,267
- Negative.
- He looks so hurt.

403
00:17:38,300 --> 00:17:41,400
- Main two.
- Negative.

404
00:17:41,433 --> 00:17:45,833
- How can he say that to me?
- Let's try the auxiliaries.

405
00:17:45,867 --> 00:17:48,667
- Main one.
- Negative.

406
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:51,967
- Main two.
- Cool, huh,
watching them check things

407
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,867
of which we don't know
what they are?

408
00:17:55,933 --> 00:17:57,633
It's not in the circuits?

409
00:17:57,667 --> 00:18:00,267
No. The meteors must've smacked
into the propulsion elements.

410
00:18:00,300 --> 00:18:03,933
- The feed lines?
- Yeah, if we're lucky.

411
00:18:03,967 --> 00:18:07,033
Well, there's only one way
to find out.

412
00:18:07,067 --> 00:18:10,300
- Looks like it.
- Yeah. Strip down, oil up.

413
00:18:12,567 --> 00:18:14,900
- You know--
- oh, boy. What?

414
00:18:14,933 --> 00:18:16,900
- ...there's one thing sure.
- What's that?

415
00:18:16,933 --> 00:18:19,000
They knew what they were doing
when they forced US

416
00:18:19,033 --> 00:18:21,333
to go through those space drills
dozens of times.

417
00:18:21,367 --> 00:18:23,700
If I remember correctly,
you had a different opinion.

418
00:18:23,733 --> 00:18:25,367
- [Chuckles]
- Don't rub it in.

419
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:28,200
That was a bad and unbeautiful
thing to say, sir.

420
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:31,367
Would it be really
that much of a problem

421
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:34,167
to have human-sized doors?

422
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,733
Lipstick of the gods.

423
00:18:41,533 --> 00:18:43,633
Man, when he starts talking
about his stamp collection,

424
00:18:43,667 --> 00:18:46,767
I just gotta get outside.

425
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:49,967
Look. There's the problem.
The dryer's leaking.

426
00:18:52,133 --> 00:18:55,400
In or out, kids? What, are we
heating the whole universe?

427
00:18:56,800 --> 00:18:59,567
I gotta get the model number
for the repairman.

428
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:04,000
I miss the earth so much.
I miss my wife.

429
00:19:04,033 --> 00:19:07,467
Uh, could you get your butt
out of my face, Ron?

430
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:11,400
Good thing there's
so much gravity out in space.

431
00:19:11,433 --> 00:19:12,867
** [ambient soundtrack music]

432
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:15,933
Soundtrack by Percy faith
and his orchestra.

433
00:19:17,967 --> 00:19:20,200
Hmm, that towel we stuffed
in that crack

434
00:19:20,233 --> 00:19:22,533
really isn't doing the trick.

435
00:19:22,567 --> 00:19:24,533
I can't believe you left
your tether ropes

436
00:19:24,567 --> 00:19:26,933
at Lloyd's cabin.

437
00:19:26,967 --> 00:19:28,933
Um, I'm not good at this, sir.

438
00:19:28,967 --> 00:19:32,267
I'm only good at fruity,
philosophical speeches.

439
00:19:34,533 --> 00:19:38,767
Don't move, Ron.
There are two giant eyeballs
on your back.

440
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:42,600
Ahh. You smell good, sir.
Like vitalis and bacon.

441
00:19:46,033 --> 00:19:48,400
Doh-ho-ho!
Doh, Stanley!

442
00:19:48,433 --> 00:19:50,400
It's a sperm whale-powered ship,
sir.

443
00:19:50,433 --> 00:19:52,200
You have to watch out
for his glow-hole.

444
00:19:52,233 --> 00:19:53,400
[Laughter]

445
00:19:53,433 --> 00:19:55,533
You're terrible astronauts.

446
00:19:58,133 --> 00:20:00,433
Oh, look.
It's the interior of our rocket.

447
00:20:00,467 --> 00:20:03,267
Cool. You can see
where we sit.

448
00:20:03,300 --> 00:20:05,267
This looks like it.

449
00:20:05,300 --> 00:20:07,900
The retro rocket feed lines
must be cut.

450
00:20:10,167 --> 00:20:13,067
The guy on the left
has a curvy puckeredesque butt.

451
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:17,933
Mosquitoes are bad tonight.

452
00:20:17,967 --> 00:20:21,467
Yeah, it used to be great
when you could fix
those rockets yourself

453
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:24,433
with just a Crescent wrench
before all that computer stuff.

454
00:20:24,467 --> 00:20:25,667
Yeah.

455
00:20:27,333 --> 00:20:29,800
Frank, your air line's broken.
Frank!

456
00:20:29,833 --> 00:20:32,333
- * motion in the ocean
- * ewww

457
00:20:32,367 --> 00:20:34,967
- * his air hose broke
- * ew, oh

458
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,200
come on, captain load.

459
00:20:37,233 --> 00:20:39,100
[Chuckles]

460
00:20:41,300 --> 00:20:43,267
[Mike, as if over radio]
I'm coming, Elizabeth.
[Static]

461
00:20:43,300 --> 00:20:45,600
Elizabeth, I'm coming.
Over.

462
00:20:45,633 --> 00:20:47,267
It's black Bart and his gang.

463
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:50,233
Get away from that rocket,
ya varmints!

464
00:20:50,267 --> 00:20:52,133
Okay. Break.
Back to position one.

465
00:20:52,167 --> 00:20:54,133
And wrestle.

466
00:20:54,167 --> 00:20:56,133
Since you're gonna
die anyway, Ron,

467
00:20:56,167 --> 00:20:58,767
I'm gonna stick you
right in the meat locker, okay?

468
00:21:01,333 --> 00:21:05,500
Uhh! Uhh!
Well, I'm glad you're safe, sir,
but could you lend me--

469
00:21:05,533 --> 00:21:08,400
oh, never mind. I'm fine.
It's okay. I'll handle it.

470
00:21:08,433 --> 00:21:11,267
Yep. Just got to, uh--
I'm fine.

471
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:13,467
If you could just prop
the door open--

472
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:15,767
nope. That's-- that's fine.

473
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:19,533
Could you just give a message
to my wife and kids,
if you have the ti--

474
00:21:19,567 --> 00:21:21,967
well, never mind.

475
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,900
Our father who art in heaven,

476
00:21:26,933 --> 00:21:29,167
hallowed by thy name...

477
00:21:40,833 --> 00:21:42,800
- Hey, servo--
- you know, Mike,

478
